<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Propaganda Ministry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reich Ministry of Public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and Propaganda was created in March 1933 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> to control German culture and public opinion. It directed the press, radio, film, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, music and art, issued daily instructions to newspapers and, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-chamber-of-culture">Reich Chamber of Culture</a>, decided who could work in the arts. It promoted the Hitler cult, antisemitism and the war effort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reich-ministry-of-public-enlightenment-and-propaganda.jpg" alt="Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sandau · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1989-0821-502,_Joseph_Goebbels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> elections of March 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> presented his cabinet with a draft resolution to establish the ministry, and he pushed it through despite the scepticism of some non-National Socialist ministers. On 13 March 1933 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> issued the decree. The ministry moved into the 18th-century Ordenspalais across from the Reich Chancellery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. On 25 March Goebbels told broadcasting directors that it had the task of carrying out an intellectual mobilization, comparable in the field of the spirit to the Ministry of Defense in the field of security. A decree of 30 June 1933 transferred numerous functions of other ministries to it, to centralise Nazi control of German cultural, media and intellectual life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The ministry began in 1933 with five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and 350 employees; by 1939 it had 2,000 employees in 17 departments, and its budget rose from 14 million Reichsmarks in 1933 to 187 million in 1941. Departments covered administration and law, propaganda, broadcasting, press, film, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, music and art, and security. Goebbels headed it with three state secretaries, including Otto Dietrich for the press from 1937.</p><p>Its work overlapped with that of other bodies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-chamber-of-culture">Reich Chamber of Culture</a> supervised creative artists, and three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> leaders with media duties, Goebbels, Max Amann and Dietrich, sometimes issued contradictory directives. The Foreign Office claimed foreign propaganda, and Hitler's directive of 8 September 1939 confirmed its leading role, though Goebbels's ministry kept interfering until the end of the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The main instrument of press control was the Reich press conference, held daily from 1 July 1933. Between 80,000 and 100,000 instructions were issued by 1945, and participants had to destroy them after use. Control combined indirect pre-censorship with direct censorship after publication. Some journalists resisted: staff of the Frankfurter Zeitung hid their notes, and Walter Schwerdtfeger was imprisoned for treason for passing instructions to the foreign press.</p><p>The film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> approved every project before shooting, and after the Reich Film Law was revised in 1934 a film could be banned for violating National Socialist, moral or artistic sensibilities. It also ran the newsreel Deutsche Wochenschau, which in 1942 reached 20 million moviegoers a week. Regional radio corporations were brought under the Reich Broadcasting Corporation in 1933, and a unified programme was broadcast from June 1940. The Flensburg Government abolished the ministry on 5 May 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Joseph Goebbels’s <b>Ministry of Propaganda</b> controlled press, radio, film and the arts through the Reich Chamber of Culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
